  "worked_solution": "Let $\\delta >0.$ A *neighborhood* of a point ${x}_{0}$ is the set of points whose distance from ${x}_{0}$ is less than $\\delta .$\n\n$$\n{x:|x-{x}_{0}|<\\delta }\n$$\n\nIn two-dimensional space, a neighborhood of ${x}_{0}$ is the open disc $|x-{x}_{0}|<\\delta ,$ as shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14041362/assets/50a6c72e169f3104eb0b9958673cc6644f770e6bcc3ebf23360eb20189325a28.png)\n\nFurthermore, we have the following definitions:\n\n- A point ${x}_{0}$ is an *interior point* of a set $S$ if $S$ contains *some* neighborhood of ${x}_{0}.$ The set of all interior points of $S$ is called the *interior* of $S.$\n\n- A point ${x}_{0}$ is a *boundary point* of a set $S$ if *every* neighborhood of ${x}_{0}$ contains points that are in $S$ and points that are not in $S.$ \nThe set of all boundary points of $S$ is called the *boundary* of $S.$\n\nThe set $A$ consists of all points that lie on or to the right of the parabola ${y}^{2}=-2x.$ A sketch of this region is shown below:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14041362/assets/e5547cb8e0ed0fec1fd2e515a1bcd9009044289dfdc516c6a6cba14b650a7645.png)\n\nLet's add the points $P(-2,2)$ and $Q(-1,0)$ and some neighborhoods of these points to our diagram.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14041362/assets/7b353dd43fd293424ba8404184e53869d41d8a1b73493d845bc85aa333130606.png)\n\nLet's examine the statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. The coordinates of $P(-2,2)$ satisfy the equation ${y}^{2}=-2x.$ Hence, $P$ lies on the boundary of the set.\n\n- Statement II is true. The coordinates of $P(-2,2)$ satisfy the inequality ${y}^{2}\\ge -2x.$ Hence, $P$ lies in the set $A.$\n\n- Statement III is false. Indeed, $Q$ is not an interior point of $A$ since any neighborhood of $Q$ contains at least one point *outside* the set.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is 'I and II only.'",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
